<Chapter id="gini" label="Measure the room" />

## Measure the room

A picture is excellent until I want to compare two rooms. Then I need a number.

Start with Gini. I like it because you can watch the number being built.

<!--
  WIDGET: GiniStage (R5, owner storyboard 2026-07-08) — the Gini built from first
  principles: equal circles merge one by one into a running total (the diagonal); keep the
  graph, deal an unequal teaching room (Gini ≈ 0.5), sort, merge again (the sagging Lorenz
  curve); hatch the gap; then a slider bends parametric Lorenz curves (L = x^k) for
  intuition. Axes are the same scale, exactly square. TOY discipline: slider rooms are
  parametric, not simulation output; the number shown is the discrete Gini of the twelve
  on-screen circles. Beat 21 discipline: introduced only for comparing whole rooms; no
  per-trade Gini claims (C5).
-->
<Deferred
  load={() => import('$lib/widgets/distribution/GiniStage.svelte')}
  label="Gini stage"
  reserve="34rem"
/>

Zero means equal. One is the theoretical horizon: as a room grows, one owner and everybody
else at nothing gets closer and closer to it.

Useful. But the question I care about is plainer: how many people still carry enough
economic weight to matter?

### Four people. How many still matter?

Four people enter with four coins each. Move the coins yourself. The people never leave.
The black box watches only where the wealth goes.

<Deferred
  load={() => import('$lib/widgets/distribution/EffectiveParticipantsStage.svelte')}
  label="effective participants stage"
  reserve="48rem"
/>

The box asks how many equal fortunes would be just as concentrated as this room. Four equal
fortunes give 4. Split everything equally between two and it gives 2. Give every coin to
one owner and it gives 1. In between, it can say 3.88 or 1.47.

That is **effective participants**. Four bodies remain in the room. The number measures the
field they have left.

<details class="claim-note">
  <summary>Open the black box</summary>
  <p>Write each person's share of the room as <em>sᵢ</em>. Square the shares, add them,
  then take the reciprocal: <em>1 / Σsᵢ²</em>. This is the inverse Herfindahl
  concentration index.</p>
</details>

### A million trades can move almost nothing

The trade counter keeps racing after most people have almost nothing left to risk. A
million crumbs still prints as a million trades.

So I count the money that crossed between people. One **measurement round** is one trade
per person on average. Add the stakes transferred in those ordinary trades and divide by
all wealth in the room. That is **ordinary turnover**. A result of 0.30 roomfuls means
wealth equal to 30% of the room changed hands during that round.

Only trades count. Later, levies and shared returns get their own ledger. The remedy does
not get to pad this number.

<Chapter id="crowd" label="More of them" />

## More of them

A small room is good for learning the instruments. Now invite a crowd: one thousand equal
fortunes, two million fair trades, fresh luck.

<!--
  WIDGET: CrowdRun — a much larger fresh population (1,000 agents, 2M trades) runs the same
  fair trades; the log-ruled histogram forms live. Gini, effective participants, and
  ordinary turnover are recorded from the same state. One measurement round is N trades;
  levy flow is absent and cannot contaminate turnover.
-->
<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/crowd/CrowdRun.svelte')} label="crowd run" reserve="26rem" />

The histogram says where the people sit. Gini says how far the room bent from equality.
Effective participants says how broad the field remains. Turnover says whether all those
trades still move meaningful wealth.

Do not ask one number to impersonate another. Read the room with all of them.

<Chapter id="where-it-ends" label="Where it ends" />

## Where it ends

That crowd was one run. A run can show you something. It cannot prove it.

Here mathematics says something stronger. Keep the room finite. Pick pairs at random. Use
one fixed stake strictly between zero and one hundred percent, and an independent fair coin.
Then continue without end. With probability one, wealth converges to a single owner.

<!--
  WIDGET: TimeLapse — time accelerates; the tail thins until one circle holds everything the
  pixels can show. Picks up the reveal room where it stopped; a fresh room ends the same way
  with a different owner.
-->
<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/ending/TimeLapse.svelte')} label="time lapse" reserve="30rem" />

The animation is not the proof. It only lets the pixels chase the theorem.

"Without end" is doing serious work. This is a limit, not a date. At any finite time the
room can be deeply concentrated without literally reaching one owner. The path can wobble,
too: a poorer circle can win and the room can briefly grow fairer on the way.

<details class="claim-note">
  <summary>Show the math</summary>
  <p>For a fixed finite number of agents and a fixed stake fraction
  <em>0 &lt; β &lt; 1</em>, the wealth vector converges almost surely to one of the room's
  one-owner states. “Almost surely” means probability one; it does not mean every imaginable
  sequence of coin tosses.</p>
  <p>The proof is in <a href="https://arxiv.org/abs/2308.01485">Börgers &amp; Greengard
  (2023)</a>.</p>
</details>

<Chapter id="dial" label="Your hand on the dial" />

## Your hand on the dial

You have seen the destination. Now put your hand on the clock.

The stake — the fraction of the poorer fortune placed on each toss — has been fixed until
now. Turn it down. Turn it up. Every change starts a fresh room, so luck is allowed to heckle
your comparison.

<!--
  WIDGET: StakeDial — dragging the stake starts a fresh room at the chosen value. Its Gini,
  effective-participant count, and ordinary turnover share one N-trade measurement clock.
  GATE(β timing): the qualitative split is safe — asymptotic concentration is the same for
  every fixed β in (0,1) (C4); the *rate* is shown as what the live runs do, not a precise
  law. Do NOT state a precise timing relationship (e.g. a formula in β or N) until the
  confirmatory grid across N, β, seeds lands (research/baseline.md). The widget's own
  footnote says "what these runs did — not a law."
-->
<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/dial/StakeDial.svelte')} label="stake dial" reserve="28rem" />

Look for the pattern across runs, not a promise from one of them. Smaller stakes usually
take longer to produce a dominant fortune. Larger stakes usually get there faster. I am not
claiming an exact stopwatch law here.

The theorem says every fixed positive stake has the same limiting destination. The dial
changes the journey, not the limit. Exactly zero is not a very slow game. It switches the
game off.

<Chapter id="stop-it" label="Now you try to stop it" />

## Now you try to stop it

Reading about it is one thing. Try to fix it yourself.

Here is a live room, falling the usual way. Your job is not to punish the largest holder.
It is to keep participation open. Point at a large holder and collect a quarter of its
wealth. Every dollar you collect returns to the room in equal shares.

<!--
  WIDGET: TaxGame — the live room keeps trading while the reader taps; tapping a circle
  levies a quarter of its current wealth and shares it back equally; the tapped circle
  flinches (display affect only). This is a manual WEALTH levy (taxes current wealth, not
  income) — named in the widget copy, C13.
  DESIGN GUARD honored: the pace is NOT tuned so the reader provably cannot win — a diligent
  hand genuinely can hold the room (robot-player tuning notes in the build log). Whether a
  fast enough hand can keep a room fair is left as the real, open question the prose asks.
-->
As you play, watch the room keep trading while you work. Can a fast enough hand preserve broad
participation, or does the room outpace the taps? The game will report what your attempt did.

<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/tax/TaxGame.svelte')} label="tax game" reserve="30rem" />

Pointing is the game, so this intervention is targeted: you choose one holder. The rule
coming next is not an automated version of your hand. It is a different rule. Nobody is
selected. The same percentage applies to every fortune, the pieces enter one pool, and the
pool returns equally to everyone.

