    23  outcome map — finite-run grid + a chosen Gini-0.5 contour; the smooth curve is labeled
        FITTED (τ ≈ c·β²). Neither the contour nor the fit is described as a phase boundary.
-->

<Chapter id="levy" label="Put the levy in the rules" />

## Put the levy in the rules

Your hands were always going to tire. So put a structural rule in the room: a proportional
wealth levy plus an equal social return, applied on its own clock.

First freeze the trading. Four fortunes are enough to see every piece move.

<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/tax/LevyLesson.svelte')} label="levy lesson" reserve="42rem" />

Collection alone would shrink every circle by the same proportion and leave every wealth
share unchanged. The equal return is what changes the shares. Below-average fortunes receive
more than they contributed; an exactly average fortune breaks even; above-average fortunes
contribute net. The room keeps every coin.

<Chapter id="tax-against-trade" label="Tax against trade" />

## Tax against trade

Now turn trading back on. To isolate the new rule, write one fresh random script of partners
and coin tosses and give it to two rooms. One gets only the trades. The other gets the same
trades plus the shared counterforce.

<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/tax/MatchedRooms.svelte')} label="matched room comparison" reserve="38rem" />

That comparison says what happened under one disclosed finite protocol. It does not choose a
real tax code, and it does not promise the same numbers on a fresh random run. It isolates one
thing: what the levy plus equal return changed while luck stayed matched.

One pair is evidence, not a landscape. So measure many. Put stake on one axis and levy rate
on the other. Every point is one finite
experiment under the same protocol; its color records the measured outcome. You fill in the
map.

<!--
  WIDGET: PhaseDiagram (legacy component name) — the reader
  runs live rooms (SandboxWorld, fresh dice, ~2.3 s each) with snap-to-grid stake/tax dials;
  each independent finished room contributes its complete outcome bundle to the shared
  evidence store and paints its cell with measured tail-mean Gini. After three
  plays, "fill in the rest" completes the 10×13 grid live in-page (two fixed seeds per
  remaining cell, chunked under a frame budget; cached per session). White contour = chosen
  finite-run Gini 0.5 outcome. Dashed curve = FITTED τ ≈ c·β². Neither is phase evidence.
-->
<Deferred load={() => import('$lib/widgets/phase/PhaseDiagram.svelte')} label="outcome map" reserve="42rem" />

If you fill the map, it adds a chosen Gini 0.5 contour and a square-law fit. The contour is
not a border between two proven phases. The fit asks a narrower question: how does the levy
needed for the same measured outcome change as the stake changes?

<Chapter id="verdict" label="So — merit, or math?" />

## So — merit, or math?

The coin is still fair.

It never saw talent, effort, inheritance, power — or even a name. Money did not buy better
odds. It bought tosses it could survive. Enough money kept one player at the table while everyone
else's chance to make a meaningful bet disappeared.

Left alone, the fair game closed itself. Fewer people could put meaningful wealth at risk,
and less wealth moved when they traded.

Then one shared counterforce kept the field open: collect the same percentage from every
fortune, put it in one pool, and return that pool equally. The coin stayed fair. More people
stayed able to play.

This room cannot tell me why any real person is rich. I threw that world away on purpose. It
can tell me something narrower: talent and effort were not required to build the trap.

So this is not an argument against merit. It is a warning about the field merit enters.

**If merit is supposed to win, keep the game open long enough for merit to play.**

I am done touching the controls.

The machine is yours. Break my argument.

## The sandbox

Everything you have seen was this one machine wearing different costumes. Here it is with
every dial exposed: people, stake, levy rate, levy timing — and the room itself answers to
your finger. It begins equal, with fresh dice. Effective participants is the first reading;
Gini is one button away. Tap anyone, change anything, and see what it takes.

